[38;2;124;124;124mthat contains infinitely many rational points?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a classic question in algebraic geometry / arithmetic geometry. The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124manswer is YES. The standard example is an elliptic curve (which is a non-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mrational irreducible algebraic curve of genus 1) with positive rank, which has [0m
[38;2;124;124;124minfinitely many rational points.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this carefully. An elliptic curve over Q is a smooth [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mprojective curve of genus 1 with a rational point. By Mordell's theorem, E(Q) is[0m[38;2;124;124;124ma finitely generated abelian group, so E(Q) ≅ Z^r ⊕ E(Q)_tors. If the rank r ≥ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1, then E(Q) is infinite.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAn elliptic curve is non-rational (a curve is rational iff genus 0, and elliptic[0m[38;2;124;124;124mcurves have genus 1). It's irreducible (smooth curves are irreducible). And it [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mhas infinitely many rational points when rank ≥ 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA concrete example: E: y² = x³ - x. This has rank... actually let me think. The [0m[38;2;124;124;124mcurve y² = x³ - x has rank 0 (it's a congruent number curve for n=1, which is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnot a congruent number). Let me pick a better one.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mE: y² = x³ + x + 1, or better, a well-known positive rank curve.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA classic example: E: y² = x³ - 4x + 4. Hmm, let me just use a well-known one.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the simplest well-known elliptic curve with positive rank: y² = x³ - [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. The point (3, 5) lies on it since 5² = 25 = 27 - 2 = 25. ✓. And this curve [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mhas rank 1 (this is a known result). The point (3,5) is of infinite order.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me verify: is (3,5) on y² = x³ - 2? 3³ - 2 = 27 - 2 = 25 = 5². [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mYes![0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTo show it's infinite order, I can compute multiples. 2(3,5) using the doubling [0m[38;2;124;124;124mformula on y² = x³ - 2 (a = 0, b = -2).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSlope m = (3x² + a)/(2y) = (3·9)/(2·5) = 27/10.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mx(2P) = m² - 2x = (27/10)² - 6 = 729/100 - 600/100 = 129/100.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124my(2P) = m(x - x(2P)) - y = (27/10)(3 - 129/100) - 5 = (27/10)(171/100) - 5 = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m4617/1000 - 5000/1000 = -383/1000.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo 2P = (129/100, -383/1000). This is a rational point, and it's not the point [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mat infinity. We can continue; since the coordinates are getting "more complex," [0m[38;2;124;124;124mP is not torsion (by the Nagell-Lutz theorem, torsion points on y² = x³ + ax + b[0m[38;2;124;124;124mwith a,b integers have integer coordinates and y² | 4a³ + 27b²). Here 4·0 + 27·4[0m[38;2;124;124;124m= 108, and y(2P) = -383/1000 is not an integer, so 2P is not torsion, hence P is[0m[38;2;124;124;124mnot torsion.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo E: y² = x³ - 2 is an irreducible (smooth) non-rational (genus 1) algebraic [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcurve with infinitely many rational points.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write a clean proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me be more careful about "irreducible." The curve y² = x³ - 2 — is[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe polynomial y² - x³ + 2 irreducible? Over Q, yes. x³ - 2 is irreducible over [0m[38;2;124;124;124mQ (Eisenstein at p=2), and y² - (x³ - 2) is irreducible over Q(x) since x³ - 2 [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mis not a square in Q(x). So the curve is irreducible.[0m

